WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.735
- You had a podcast not long ago

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:02.902
where you were talking about

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:05.672 align:start size:84% position:16%
how Dawkins basically
said that, well,

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:08.708
religion does have some
benefits to society.

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:11.077
And I think he's realizing too

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.679
that in Britain in particular,

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:18.752 align:start size:97% position:3%
a tepid secularism is not
gonna resist a radical Islam,

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.389 align:start size:97% position:3%
but Christianity could be
just from a pragmatic point,

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:24.891
point of view, from
his point of view,

00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:26.993
can maybe blunt radical Islam.

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:31.397 align:start size:97% position:3%
So he's realizing there's some
pragmatic uses to religion.

00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:36.469
But I think originally the
new atheism came out of 9/11,

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.039
these fundamentalists,
you know the old saying

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:42.075
that science will teach
you how to fly a plane,

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:43.343
but religion will teach you

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:44.911
how to fly a plane
into a building.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.413
You know, that kind of thing.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:49.482 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so they were really
adamant against any religion,

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:52.185
and they would put
'em all together.

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:56.556
Islam, Christianity, all these
religions are just basically

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:59.225 align:start size:84% position:16%
looked at with
disdain at the time.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.060
And I think people
starting to realize

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.262
that's not really the
way the world works.

00:01:03.262 --> 00:01:04.764
That's not really true.

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:05.965
These religions are different,

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:07.900 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they're different
for good reasons.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.970
(light jazzy music)

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.809
- All right, we are
at the local studio

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:18.411
here in Miami, Florida,

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:20.846 align:start size:84% position:16%
and joining me
today is the author

00:01:20.846 --> 00:01:23.683
of "I Don't Have Enough
Faith to Be An Atheist."

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.150
Great title, man.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.052 align:start size:84% position:16%
Frank Turek,
pleasure to meet you.

00:01:27.052 --> 00:01:28.922
- Dave, it is a pleasure.
Great being here with you.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.489
- Thanks for coming to town.

00:01:30.489 --> 00:01:31.925 align:start size:94% position:6%
You've come from
Charlotte, North Carolina.

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:33.159
- Yes, sir.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.660
- I appreciate you
making the trek.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.629
Let's start with the
title of this book.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:40.200 align:start size:88% position:13%
"I Don't Have Enough
Faith to Be An Atheist."

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.902
- I didn't think of
it. My co-author did.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.238
- So I should say you are
technically the co-author

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:46.239
of this book.

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:47.640
Tell me a little bit about your.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.809
- Well, Dr. Norman Geisler,

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:52.412
when he died, he
died in 2019, Dave,

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:54.414
they added up the number
of books he either wrote,

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:57.851
co-wrote or updated, it was 129.

00:01:57.851 --> 00:02:00.253
The man had written more books,

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:02.121
not only than most
people have read,

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:03.489
than most people have seen,

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:05.191 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause who goes to
library anymore, right?

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.725
This guy was brilliant,

00:02:06.725 --> 00:02:09.195
and at one point he and I
were traveling the country

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:10.596
doing a talk we called

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:12.932 align:start size:88% position:13%
"The 12 Points That Show
Christianity's True."

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:16.302
And he went through the
fine-tuning argument

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:19.905
that the universe is fine
tuned so incomprehensibly,

00:02:19.905 --> 00:02:24.143
and after he got done with
this amazing presentation,

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:26.745
he finally said,
"In light of this,

00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:28.848
I just don't have enough
faith to be an atheist."

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:30.849
And I went, "That needs to
be the title of the book."

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:32.485
- So it really was
a throwaway line.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:33.686
- Yeah, he just said,

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:34.921 align:start size:88% position:13%
"I don't have enough
faith to be an atheist."

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.522 align:start size:84% position:16%
So he said, "Well,
let's write the book."

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:38.891
So we wrote the book based
on the seminar we were doing.

00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:40.627 align:start size:94% position:6%
We sent it to the
publisher, and they said,

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:41.961
"We don't like the title."

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:44.464 align:start size:81% position:19%
I said, "You don't
like the title?

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:45.798 align:start size:84% position:16%
What do you think
the title should be?"

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:48.301
And they said, "The
Truth About Truth."

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:50.270 align:start size:84% position:16%
I said, "Sorry,
that's a deal breaker.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:51.538 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're going to
another publisher."

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:53.339
They said, "Okay, we'll
stick with the title."

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:54.507
And thankfully they did.

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.743
- And the book came out in 2004.

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:58.178
Tell me a little bit

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.579 align:start size:88% position:13%
about how you two
linked up your journeys

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:00.779
to get to the book,

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:02.881
and then we'll dive into
obviously the specifics

00:03:02.881 --> 00:03:05.451 align:start size:97% position:3%
of why you don't have enough
faith to be an atheist.

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:07.019
- I was in the Navy,

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:08.588
which stands for never
again volunteer yourself,

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:09.956
for eight years.

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:13.459 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, but I came to
faith by reading books

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
by a man by the name
of Josh McDowell.

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:18.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was brought up in New
Jersey, so I was Catholic,

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:20.567
'cause as you know, in
Jersey it's the law.

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:23.069 align:start size:84% position:16%
You're either Catholic
or Jewish, right?

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.137
I went to Catholic high school,

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:25.471
but I never knew who Jesus was.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:27.607
And when I was in the Navy,

00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:29.775
I met the son of a
Methodist minister,

00:03:29.775 --> 00:03:31.277
and I had so many
questions for me.

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:34.780 align:start size:97% position:3%
He said, "Frank, you just need
to get Josh McDowell books,

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.916 align:start size:97% position:3%
evidenced demands a verdict
more than a carpenter."

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:38.551
So I read those books,

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:42.221
and I began to realize that
Christianity is indeed true.

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:43.789
When I got out of the Navy,

00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:46.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
I happened to meet Norman
Geisler, and he said,

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.594 align:start size:84% position:16%
"I'm starting a
seminary in Charlotte.

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:50.430 align:start size:88% position:13%
Why don't you come
down and check it out?"

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.998
So we did, and six months later,

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:53.465
we moved the whole family there.

00:03:53.465 --> 00:03:54.834 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Did you consider yourself
a believer before that?

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:56.402
Or you were just sort
of nominally Catholic

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.371
or just secular northeast.

00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:01.741
- I became a believer in the
Navy by reading these books

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.743
and then starting
to go to church.

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:05.878
And then when I met Geisler,
who I didn't know at the time,

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.814
but to use a dated reference,

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:10.082 align:start size:84% position:16%
he was the Michael
Jordan of Apologetics,

00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:13.052
what we call evidence for
the faith, at the time.

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:14.954
And when I met him and
looked him up, I said,

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:18.023
"If I wanna get into this
field, this is the guy."

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:19.191
Right?

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:20.360 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, it's
like in philosophy,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:21.794
your friend Jordan Peterson,

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:23.930
if he was still at the
University of Toronto,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.232
and he was still teaching,
and you wanted to learn,

00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:27.933
that's the guy, right?

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:31.136
He was the Jordan Peterson
of Christian Apologetics.

00:04:31.136 --> 00:04:33.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So the reason that I
wanted to have you on now,

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.141
and my producer Phoenix,

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:36.575
has been mentioning you
for quite some time,

00:04:36.575 --> 00:04:39.044 align:start position:0%
is that there seems to be
something interesting happening

00:04:39.044 --> 00:04:42.214
with the atheist movement,
if we can call it a movement.

00:04:42.214 --> 00:04:45.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, in essence, it has
basically fallen apart,

00:04:45.651 --> 00:04:48.921 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, from Sam Harris sort
of disappearing off Twitter

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:51.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
and having, you know, a
lot of political problems.

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.826
That whole four horseman
thing sort of disappeared.

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:55.261
The new atheist movement,

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:56.795
I don't know if you've seen

00:04:56.795 --> 00:04:59.565
that the president of the, I
think, Atheists of America,

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:01.867 align:start size:97% position:3%
David Silverman, has
basically come out and said

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:03.903 align:start size:88% position:13%
that the atheist
movement was a failure.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:06.339
This is just in the last
two or three weeks or so,

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:08.775 align:start size:97% position:3%
I've seen other, a well-known
atheists like skeptic,

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:10.576
my friend Michael Schirmer,

00:05:10.576 --> 00:05:14.113 align:start position:0%
talk about how there is a
purpose and a need for religion.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:15.848
People fill that up
with something else.

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:17.283
And of course, Jordan Peterson

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:18.918 align:start size:88% position:13%
talking about how
people end up believing

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:20.419
whether they believe it or not.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:23.155
So there's something
interesting culturally

00:05:23.155 --> 00:05:24.423
happening right now,

00:05:24.423 --> 00:05:26.325
which is sort of what I
wanted to bring you on.

00:05:26.325 --> 00:05:28.093 align:start size:88% position:13%
I wonder if, if you have
any thoughts on that,

00:05:28.093 --> 00:05:29.495
just sort of what's
happening right now?

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:31.864
- I think that the new
atheist movement, Dave,

00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:36.469
was a reaction to 9/11,
because at the time,

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:39.872
the new atheists at the time
were Christopher Hitchens.

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:42.074 align:start size:97% position:3%
I had a couple of
opportunities to debate him

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:44.210
in the late 2000s.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:47.980
Also, Richard Dawkins and
Sam Harris, Dan Dennett.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.581
- Even Dawkins, I don't
dunno if you've seen it

00:05:49.581 --> 00:05:50.816
in the last couple
of week, has said.

00:05:50.816 --> 00:05:52.318 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, you had a
podcast not long ago

00:05:52.318 --> 00:05:55.320
where you were talking about
how Dawkins basically said

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.292 align:start size:97% position:3%
that, well, religion does
have some benefits to society.

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:01.260
And I think he's realizing too

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.096
that in Britain in particular,

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:08.735 align:start size:97% position:3%
a tepid secularism is not
gonna resist a radical Islam,

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:13.806 align:start size:97% position:3%
but Christianity could be
just from a pragmatic point,

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:15.908
point of view, from
his point of view,

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:17.610
can maybe blunt radical Islam.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:21.747 align:start size:97% position:3%
So he's realizing there's some
pragmatic uses to religion.

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:26.819
But I think originally the
new atheism came out of 9/11.

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:29.421
These fundamentalists, you
know, you know the old saying

00:06:29.421 --> 00:06:32.258
that science will teach
you how to fly a plane,

00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:33.726
but religion will teach you

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:35.027
how to fly a plane
into a building.

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:36.462
You know, that kind of thing.

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:39.498 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so they were really
adamant against any religion,

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:42.802
and they would plot, or they
would put 'em all together.

00:06:42.802 --> 00:06:47.473
Islam, Christianity, all these
religions are just basically

00:06:47.473 --> 00:06:49.842 align:start size:84% position:16%
looked at with
disdain at the time.

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:51.811
And I think people
starting to realize

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:53.779
that's not really the
way the world works.

00:06:53.779 --> 00:06:55.381
That's not really true.

00:06:55.381 --> 00:06:56.582
These religions are different,

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:58.150 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they're different
for good reasons.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.687 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, so it's interesting
because what you're describing

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:04.490 align:start size:94% position:6%
as the push, the
Christian sort of argument

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.092 align:start size:84% position:16%
as the pushback
against radical Islam,

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:09.295
I think what we have here is
the push back against wokism,

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:11.730
that people, and this
is what David Silverman

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:14.132
from the American
atheist was saying.

00:07:14.132 --> 00:07:17.536
He did not realize that
wokism was gonna become,

00:07:17.536 --> 00:07:19.906
I think he called it a
cult, not a religion.

00:07:19.906 --> 00:07:21.807 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think that's
sort of what brings us

00:07:21.807 --> 00:07:23.342
to this moment in America.

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:26.679
- Yes, and Silverman was the
president, American atheist.

00:07:26.679 --> 00:07:27.913
In fact, I had a debate with him

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:30.216
probably seven or
eight years ago,

00:07:30.216 --> 00:07:32.117
but then he left that role.

00:07:32.117 --> 00:07:33.286 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't even know
what he's doing now.

00:07:33.286 --> 00:07:34.487
I mean, the whole movement

00:07:34.487 --> 00:07:37.256
maybe has pretty much
evaporated really.

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:39.892 align:start size:84% position:16%
Hitchens, of
course, passed away.

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:43.396 align:start size:97% position:3%
Dawkins is starting to realize
there's utility to religion

00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:45.097
from pragmatic point of view.

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:48.000 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I know it's gonna
sound odd, Dave,

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.135 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I have a lot of
respect for Richard Dawkins

00:07:50.135 --> 00:07:53.706
because he has more courage
than many American pastors.

00:07:53.706 --> 00:07:55.875
I mean, Richard Dawkins
has spoke against wokism.

00:07:55.875 --> 00:07:57.542
Richard Dawkins has said,

00:07:57.542 --> 00:07:59.778
"I'm sorry, there's only
two genders. It's science."

00:07:59.778 --> 00:08:01.246
Right?
- Yeah.

00:08:01.246 --> 00:08:03.415 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Richard Dawkins has
talked about the problems

00:08:03.415 --> 00:08:04.917
with radical Islam.

00:08:04.917 --> 00:08:07.520
Most pastors are hiding under
their desks on these issues.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.355
They're not coming out
and talking about this.

00:08:09.355 --> 00:08:12.558 align:start size:97% position:3%
So to his credit, Richard
Dawkins has said some things

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:14.627
that I wish American
pastors would say.

00:08:14.627 --> 00:08:16.161
- So what's going on there?

00:08:16.161 --> 00:08:17.829
Let's dive into that a little
because it's not just pastors.

00:08:17.829 --> 00:08:20.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, this is happening
throughout Jewish synagogues.

00:08:20.932 --> 00:08:22.635
It's happening through
almost every denomination

00:08:22.635 --> 00:08:25.170 align:start size:88% position:13%
of Christianity where
the rabbis, the pastors,

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.039
they're choosing wokism,

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:31.110
they're choosing equity
over religious teachings.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.544
- Right, right, well.

00:08:32.544 --> 00:08:34.846 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Thinking it was gonna bring
more people in, I think.

00:08:34.846 --> 00:08:36.315
But it seems to be
doing the reverse.

00:08:36.315 --> 00:08:38.684
- Yeah, I don't
know if, I think,

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:41.186 align:start size:84% position:16%
Christians have
bought into the idea

00:08:41.186 --> 00:08:44.356
that politics is sort of
off the table for them.

00:08:44.356 --> 00:08:48.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
What they don't seem to
realize is that, first of all,

00:08:48.594 --> 00:08:50.863 align:start size:84% position:16%
their ability to
actually be Christians

00:08:50.863 --> 00:08:52.164
and live the Christian faith

00:08:52.164 --> 00:08:54.332 align:start size:84% position:16%
and preach the
gospel, so to speak,

00:08:54.332 --> 00:08:58.136
is determined to a certain
extent by what laws are made.

00:08:58.136 --> 00:08:59.338
Right?

00:08:59.338 --> 00:09:00.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, here we
take it for granted

00:09:00.273 --> 00:09:01.507
we have religious freedom,

00:09:01.507 --> 00:09:02.741
but as you've pointed
out and many others,

00:09:02.741 --> 00:09:05.377
it's evaporating, right?

00:09:05.377 --> 00:09:07.045
So for no other reason,

00:09:07.045 --> 00:09:09.581
Christians ought to be
involved in politics,

00:09:09.581 --> 00:09:12.851
is to protect the variability
to preach and live the gospel.

00:09:12.851 --> 00:09:14.086
The second reason is,

00:09:14.086 --> 00:09:16.255
I always ask Christians
this or anybody this,

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:18.658
should Christians care
how people are treated?

00:09:18.658 --> 00:09:19.925 align:start size:81% position:19%
Everyone says,
"Well, of course."

00:09:19.925 --> 00:09:21.226
Well, should Christians care

00:09:21.226 --> 00:09:23.763
how people are treated
by their government?

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:25.064
Well, yeah, that follows.

00:09:25.064 --> 00:09:28.300
Now suddenly you better
care about politics.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:30.736 align:start size:88% position:13%
You better care about
the rules that are made.

00:09:30.736 --> 00:09:32.672 align:start size:81% position:19%
Because if you
care about people,

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:34.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
you've gotta care
about what's going on.

00:09:34.472 --> 00:09:37.943 align:start size:97% position:3%
And thankfully, you and
others have been a champion

00:09:37.943 --> 00:09:40.512 align:start size:84% position:16%
for saying this
transgenderism issue,

00:09:40.512 --> 00:09:43.548 align:start size:94% position:6%
particularly on children,
should be off limits.

00:09:43.548 --> 00:09:46.052 align:start size:97% position:3%
And pastors even are afraid
to talk about it, Dave.

00:09:46.052 --> 00:09:47.253
It's crazy.

00:09:47.253 --> 00:09:50.522
- I know it's crazy,
and I see it every day.

00:09:50.522 --> 00:09:52.925 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's why one of the
things that's been interesting

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:55.661 align:start size:84% position:16%
for me as someone
who's not Christian,

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:58.297 align:start size:88% position:13%
and certainly a certain
set of Christians

00:09:58.297 --> 00:10:00.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
might have some issues
with my lifestyle

00:10:00.832 --> 00:10:02.334
and everything else.

00:10:02.334 --> 00:10:04.436 align:start size:88% position:13%
I have found
generally, especially
evangelical Christians,

00:10:04.436 --> 00:10:07.072
to be the most welcoming,
decent people out there,

00:10:07.072 --> 00:10:09.008 align:start size:81% position:19%
which did help me,
I think, evolve

00:10:09.008 --> 00:10:10.576
in some ways in my thinking.

00:10:10.576 --> 00:10:14.046
- Yeah, you had a, I
just re-listened to it.

00:10:14.046 --> 00:10:15.814
It was four years ago,
that dialogue you had

00:10:15.814 --> 00:10:18.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
with my friend John
Lennox and Justin Briley.

00:10:18.483 --> 00:10:20.252 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Lennox.

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:22.087
- That was a great event.

00:10:22.087 --> 00:10:23.990
- If every Christian
was like John Lennox,

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:25.524 align:start size:84% position:16%
the whole world would
be Christianized,

00:10:25.524 --> 00:10:27.360 align:start size:88% position:13%
because you can't not
like John Lennox, right?

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:28.861
- He's just a fun guy, smiling.

00:10:28.861 --> 00:10:30.563
He's telling you this and
jokey, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:30.563 --> 00:10:32.531
- He's like your favorite uncle

00:10:32.531 --> 00:10:34.333
who has all these great stories,

00:10:34.333 --> 00:10:37.970
and John is just one of
the guys I look up to

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:41.507 align:start size:97% position:3%
in my field of work because
he is just so affable

00:10:41.507 --> 00:10:43.009
but yet so intelligent.

00:10:43.009 --> 00:10:45.411 align:start size:97% position:3%
He's a PhD in mathematics
and philosophy. He's amazing.

00:10:45.411 --> 00:10:46.946
- So, okay.

00:10:46.946 --> 00:10:48.180
So I would just wanna return
to the title for a moment.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:49.749 align:start size:88% position:13%
"I don't have enough
faith to be an atheist."

00:10:49.749 --> 00:10:52.284
I think from an American sort
of broad cultural perspective,

00:10:52.284 --> 00:10:54.887
most people, it's not
that they're atheist,

00:10:54.887 --> 00:10:56.789 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they just sort of don't
know what they believe.

00:10:56.789 --> 00:10:58.791
We have just sort
of a set of things

00:10:58.791 --> 00:11:00.359 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we kind of wake
up to every day,

00:11:00.359 --> 00:11:02.661
a culture war, a political fire,

00:11:02.661 --> 00:11:05.130
and then that's kind of
what they believe in,

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:08.000 align:start position:0%
the thing that's happening sort
of every day at the moment.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.835 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think that
sort of leads people

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:11.303
to a degree of craziness.

00:11:11.303 --> 00:11:13.872
So how would you unfurl, if you
think that's a fair premise,

00:11:13.872 --> 00:11:15.740 align:start size:94% position:6%
how would you kind of
unfurl people out of that?

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:20.045 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, there's two questions
that need to be answered.

00:11:20.045 --> 00:11:23.916
Does God exist, and if
so, what has he said?

00:11:23.916 --> 00:11:26.018
Those are the two big questions.

00:11:26.018 --> 00:11:27.385
- All right, let's do it.

00:11:27.385 --> 00:11:28.220 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Right?
- Okay, let's go.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:29.821
- So does God exist?

00:11:29.821 --> 00:11:32.458
I think when somebody asks
me, how do I know God exists?

00:11:32.458 --> 00:11:34.860
I say, "I know God
by his effects."

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:36.996 align:start size:84% position:16%
If there's a creation,
and there is,

00:11:36.996 --> 00:11:38.264
this universe had a beginning

00:11:38.264 --> 00:11:39.632
as even atheists are admitting,

00:11:39.632 --> 00:11:41.834
then there has to be a creator.

00:11:41.834 --> 00:11:44.670
The creation is the effect
that causes the creator.

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:46.272
- So they might say
it's the Big Bang,

00:11:46.272 --> 00:11:47.673
and it was just a bunch of gases

00:11:47.673 --> 00:11:49.475
and this, well, they
wouldn't say miracle,

00:11:49.475 --> 00:11:51.377
but they would say just
this thing happened,

00:11:51.377 --> 00:11:53.345
this causeless thing happened.

00:11:53.345 --> 00:11:55.214
- They will say that,
but it seems to me

00:11:55.214 --> 00:11:58.050
that if space, time and
matter had a beginning,

00:11:58.050 --> 00:11:59.819 align:start size:97% position:3%
the only thing that could
have caused that is something

00:11:59.819 --> 00:12:01.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
that transcends space,
time and matter.

00:12:01.587 --> 00:12:04.990 align:start size:97% position:3%
In other words, the cause
must be spaceless, timeless,

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:08.360
immaterial, powerful to create
the universe out of nothing,

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:10.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
personal in order
to choose to create.

00:12:10.762 --> 00:12:13.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because impersonal forces
don't make choices.

00:12:13.098 --> 00:12:14.500
Only a person can make a choice

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:18.303
to go from nothingness
to a state of creation.

00:12:18.303 --> 00:12:20.139
The cause would also
have to be intelligent

00:12:20.139 --> 00:12:21.841
to have a mind to make a choice.

00:12:21.841 --> 00:12:23.342
So I always ask people, I say,

00:12:23.342 --> 00:12:26.344 align:start position:0%
"When you think about a
spaceless, timeless, immaterial,

00:12:26.344 --> 00:12:30.749
powerful, personal, intelligent
cause, who do you think of?"

00:12:30.749 --> 00:12:32.818
And most people say God,
but then they'll say, well,

00:12:32.818 --> 00:12:34.886
how do you know it's
the Christian god,
and the answer is,

00:12:34.886 --> 00:12:36.989
we don't know if it's the
Christian god, unless,

00:12:36.989 --> 00:12:39.391
because this god could be Allah

00:12:39.391 --> 00:12:41.527 align:start size:84% position:16%
or some other
theistic god, right?

00:12:41.527 --> 00:12:43.328
Unless Jesus rose from the dead.

00:12:43.328 --> 00:12:44.930
And if you can discover

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:47.032
that Jesus really did
rise from the dead,

00:12:47.032 --> 00:12:49.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
then we can say
that the same being

00:12:49.201 --> 00:12:52.705
that walked out of the
tomb 1,990 years ago

00:12:52.705 --> 00:12:54.273
is the same being

00:12:54.273 --> 00:12:57.176
who's divine nature created
the universe out of nothing.

00:12:57.176 --> 00:12:58.711
So you've gotta,

00:12:58.711 --> 00:13:01.346
you don't get all the way
to Jesus from one argument.

00:13:01.346 --> 00:13:04.316 align:start size:97% position:3%
But if you can show that
the universe had a beginning

00:13:04.316 --> 00:13:07.119 align:start size:84% position:16%
and had a beginner
with those attributes,

00:13:07.119 --> 00:13:08.920
and you can see that
Jesus rose in the dead,

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:10.388
then you can show that
Christianity is true.

00:13:10.388 --> 00:13:12.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's just one of
several arguments for God.

00:13:12.424 --> 00:13:14.326
- Yeah, feel free to
make a couple others.

00:13:14.326 --> 00:13:15.561 align:start size:88% position:13%
Why don't we go through
a couple others?

00:13:15.561 --> 00:13:17.629
- Yeah, the second
is the teleological

00:13:17.629 --> 00:13:18.997
or the design argument,

00:13:18.997 --> 00:13:23.335
which is so incredibly
difficult to explain

00:13:23.335 --> 00:13:25.037
from an atheistic perspective

00:13:25.037 --> 00:13:26.906 align:start size:84% position:16%
that even Christopher
Hitchens said,

00:13:26.906 --> 00:13:29.742 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't know how to
explain this one, right?

00:13:29.742 --> 00:13:31.943 align:start size:84% position:16%
For example, the
gravitational force,

00:13:31.943 --> 00:13:35.580 align:start position:0%
if it were altered by more
than 1 in 10 to the 40th power,

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.784 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's one part in one
with 40 zeros following it,

00:13:38.784 --> 00:13:40.186
we wouldn't exist.

00:13:40.186 --> 00:13:42.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
An illustration I
like to give is this.

00:13:42.854 --> 00:13:45.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you were to take the entire
North American continent

00:13:45.858 --> 00:13:48.227 align:start size:88% position:13%
from Central America all
the way to Greenland,

00:13:48.227 --> 00:13:51.863 align:start size:88% position:13%
stack it in dimes to
the moon, 238,000 miles,

00:13:51.863 --> 00:13:56.435 align:start position:0%
then do that on a billion other
North American continents.

00:13:56.435 --> 00:13:58.571
Take all those dimes,
put 'em in one pile,

00:13:58.571 --> 00:14:01.507
mark one dime red, mix it
in, blindfold a friend,

00:14:01.507 --> 00:14:03.508 align:start size:84% position:16%
throw 'em on the pile,
say pick one dime.

00:14:03.508 --> 00:14:05.377
The chances he would
pick that one red dime

00:14:05.377 --> 00:14:07.980
is one chance in 10
to the 40th power.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:10.549
He's not gonna pick
that dime, right?

00:14:10.549 --> 00:14:13.318
So the implication here is,

00:14:13.318 --> 00:14:15.454 align:start position:0%
and this is just one of several
factors about the universe,

00:14:15.454 --> 00:14:17.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
change any one of
them, we're not here.

00:14:17.423 --> 00:14:20.159
Is that what best explains that?

00:14:20.159 --> 00:14:23.161 align:start size:84% position:16%
Chance, whatever that
means, or design.

00:14:23.161 --> 00:14:26.732
I mean, either this value
was designed or it wasn't.

00:14:26.732 --> 00:14:29.001 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it seems, if we're
gonna be rational,

00:14:29.001 --> 00:14:30.535
we have to say it's designed.

00:14:30.535 --> 00:14:32.638
And as I say, it's
just one of several.

00:14:32.638 --> 00:14:34.940
So that's one aspect
of the design argument.

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:37.375
Then when you get to
biology, and you see

00:14:37.375 --> 00:14:39.377
that in every one of your
a hundred trillion cells,

00:14:39.377 --> 00:14:43.248
there's a software program
3.5 billion letters long.

00:14:43.248 --> 00:14:46.351
I mean, if we were to go
out to the beach right here

00:14:46.351 --> 00:14:49.688
right now, Dave, and we're
walking along Miami Beach,

00:14:49.688 --> 00:14:52.190 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it says, "John
loves Mary" in the sand.

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:53.959
We wouldn't go, oh,
the waves did that.

00:14:53.959 --> 00:14:56.127
Or, you know, crabs
came out of the water

00:14:56.127 --> 00:14:57.562
and made that message.

00:14:57.562 --> 00:15:01.533 align:start size:97% position:3%
No, we'd say that that message
had to come from a mind.

00:15:01.533 --> 00:15:03.802 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, what happens
when we find a message

00:15:03.802 --> 00:15:05.837
that's 3.5 billion letters long

00:15:05.837 --> 00:15:08.607
in every one of our a
hundred trillion cells?

00:15:08.607 --> 00:15:11.677
If John loves Mary
requires a mind,

00:15:11.677 --> 00:15:15.714
doesn't a message 3.5 billion
letters long require a mind?

00:15:15.714 --> 00:15:19.651
Seems to me that's an effect
that needs a cause like God.

00:15:19.651 --> 00:15:20.853
And then of course,

00:15:20.853 --> 00:15:22.988
the third argument that
we often talk about,

00:15:22.988 --> 00:15:26.124
which is probably most germane
to the topics you talk about,

00:15:26.124 --> 00:15:27.826
is the moral argument.

00:15:27.826 --> 00:15:29.061
Because if there's no God,

00:15:29.061 --> 00:15:30.796
everything's just a
matter of opinion.

00:15:30.796 --> 00:15:32.331
There's no standard beyond us,

00:15:32.331 --> 00:15:35.901 align:start size:88% position:13%
no transcendent standard
of righteousness

00:15:35.901 --> 00:15:38.036
that we're obligated to obey,

00:15:38.036 --> 00:15:41.573 align:start position:0%
then there's no difference
between Mother Teresa and Hitler

00:15:41.573 --> 00:15:43.308
from a moral perspective, right?

00:15:43.308 --> 00:15:45.878 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's just your opinion against
somebody else's opinion.

00:15:45.878 --> 00:15:47.746
And so that argument, I think,

00:15:47.746 --> 00:15:50.649
probably carries the most
weight with people today.

00:15:50.649 --> 00:15:52.750
- Right, so is that
the one you think

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:55.754 align:start size:97% position:3%
that maybe led us to so
much of the craziness today,

00:15:55.754 --> 00:15:56.989
when we're debating,

00:15:56.989 --> 00:15:58.290
as my friend Douglas
Murray often says,

00:15:58.290 --> 00:15:59.958 align:start size:84% position:16%
things that we've
put to bed years ago,

00:15:59.958 --> 00:16:01.694
and suddenly we're
debating, you know,

00:16:01.694 --> 00:16:03.561
whether boys or girls
are girls or boys,

00:16:03.561 --> 00:16:07.265
it's because we have sort
of no moral basis anymore

00:16:07.265 --> 00:16:09.067
at scale in society.

00:16:09.067 --> 00:16:11.169 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, yeah, it's
a free-for-all.

00:16:11.169 --> 00:16:14.505
But the very people that are
arguing for that are arguing

00:16:14.505 --> 00:16:17.042 align:start size:97% position:3%
as if there's a moral right
to do this, you notice,

00:16:17.042 --> 00:16:19.478
because everybody's trying
to oppose a moral position.

00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:21.713
The only question is
who's moral position?

00:16:21.713 --> 00:16:22.981 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now, all laws
legislate morality.

00:16:22.981 --> 00:16:24.816
The only question
is whose morality?

00:16:24.816 --> 00:16:27.219
And what I try and
say to people is,

00:16:27.219 --> 00:16:28.920 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't wanna
legislate my morality,

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:30.489 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't wanna legislate
your morality,

00:16:30.489 --> 00:16:31.857
I wanna legislate the morality,

00:16:31.857 --> 00:16:34.693
the one Thomas Jefferson
said was self-evident.

00:16:34.693 --> 00:16:36.728
So that self-evident morality

00:16:36.728 --> 00:16:39.030
upon which this nation
was found is the morality

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:40.832
we ought to get back to,

00:16:40.832 --> 00:16:43.135
even though they were
inconsistent with it.

00:16:43.135 --> 00:16:45.037 align:start size:84% position:16%
Obviously on slavery
and other issues,

00:16:45.037 --> 00:16:47.438
they were inconsistent, but
they knew in their hearts,

00:16:47.438 --> 00:16:49.674
in fact, Jefferson and
the founding fathers knew

00:16:49.674 --> 00:16:51.110
that slavery was
gonna be an issue,

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:52.745
that it wasn't morally right.

00:16:52.745 --> 00:16:54.012
They just couldn't figure out

00:16:54.012 --> 00:16:55.414
how to get rid of
it at the time.

00:16:55.414 --> 00:16:56.781
- As I tell people all the time,

00:16:56.781 --> 00:16:58.617 align:start size:97% position:3%
he was writing the
documents to free the slaves

00:16:58.617 --> 00:17:00.485
as he himself was a slave owner.

00:17:00.485 --> 00:17:03.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
He was just a man of his
time, as incredible as he was.

00:17:03.922 --> 00:17:05.424
What would you say to the line,

00:17:05.424 --> 00:17:08.126
one of the things that I think
shifted me on a lot of this,

00:17:08.126 --> 00:17:11.363
Jordan Peterson, you know,
when we toured together,

00:17:11.363 --> 00:17:13.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
it would come up literally
every single night.

00:17:13.999 --> 00:17:17.302
People would say, "Please
define God, explain God.

00:17:17.302 --> 00:17:18.504
How did you become a believer?"

00:17:18.504 --> 00:17:20.339
And, and the short answer,

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:22.006
he really didn't
like that question.

00:17:22.006 --> 00:17:23.675
Not because he didn't
think it was valuable,

00:17:23.675 --> 00:17:26.044
but because it was just
coming up all the time.

00:17:26.044 --> 00:17:28.413 align:start position:0%
And he felt that there's so
many other things to talk about

00:17:28.413 --> 00:17:31.316
and everything else, but the
simplest version of it would be

00:17:31.316 --> 00:17:35.988
that he behaves as
if God exists, that's
what he would say.

00:17:35.988 --> 00:17:39.224 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I thought that
that was pretty solid,

00:17:39.224 --> 00:17:41.527
that that was almost
enough for most people.

00:17:41.527 --> 00:17:43.328
Do you think that that's fair?

00:17:43.328 --> 00:17:44.996
- Well, the question-

00:17:44.996 --> 00:17:47.399
- That he can't sit there and
tell you, okay, God exists,

00:17:47.399 --> 00:17:49.634 align:start size:81% position:19%
but he will behave
in such a way.

00:17:49.634 --> 00:17:53.939
- Right, I think Jordan takes
more of a utilitarian approach

00:17:53.939 --> 00:17:56.575 align:start size:84% position:16%
to God, that whether
he exists or not,

00:17:56.575 --> 00:17:58.042
we better believe in him.

00:17:58.042 --> 00:18:00.345 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because if we don't, it's
gonna be, as Dostoevsky said,

00:18:00.345 --> 00:18:02.881
you know, if if there is no God,

00:18:02.881 --> 00:18:04.949
everything's permissible, right?

00:18:04.949 --> 00:18:07.652
It's gonna be chaos as
Nietzsche pointed out.

00:18:07.652 --> 00:18:11.189
However, I think there's
evidence that God exists,

00:18:11.189 --> 00:18:13.258
and I think you can show
beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:18:13.258 --> 00:18:16.128
Not beyond all doubt. I
could be wrong, right?

00:18:16.128 --> 00:18:17.996
But I think that God does exist.

00:18:17.996 --> 00:18:19.431
And if Jesus rose from the dead,

00:18:19.431 --> 00:18:21.333
and I think we can give
evidence that he did,

00:18:21.333 --> 00:18:23.635
then the Christian
god is the true god.

00:18:23.635 --> 00:18:26.371
So if that's the
case, who is God?

00:18:26.371 --> 00:18:30.242 align:start position:0%
God in the abstract is
spaceless, timeless, immaterial,

00:18:30.242 --> 00:18:32.611
powerful, personal
and intelligent.

00:18:32.611 --> 00:18:35.047
In the flesh, he's Jesus.

00:18:35.047 --> 00:18:37.482
He adds humanity to his deity.

00:18:37.482 --> 00:18:39.084
And he comes to Earth,

00:18:39.084 --> 00:18:42.754 align:start size:88% position:13%
not to give us some
sort of new moral code,

00:18:42.754 --> 00:18:45.290
but to be our substitute.

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:48.259
He is the one that lives the
perfect life in our place.

00:18:48.259 --> 00:18:50.328
So by trusting in him,

00:18:50.328 --> 00:18:54.166 align:start size:88% position:13%
we can have our moral
transgressions forgiven,

00:18:54.166 --> 00:18:56.667
and we can be given
his righteousness.

00:18:56.667 --> 00:18:59.838
So Christianity, contrary
to what many will say,

00:18:59.838 --> 00:19:02.774
is not a system that tries
to get you to live better.

00:19:02.774 --> 00:19:04.309
Christianity is a system

00:19:04.309 --> 00:19:06.778
that tries to get you to
accept the substitute.

00:19:06.778 --> 00:19:09.815 align:start size:84% position:16%
And because you do
accept the substitute,

00:19:09.815 --> 00:19:12.451
out of gratitude for
what he's done for you,

00:19:12.451 --> 00:19:14.419
then you will live a moral life.

00:19:14.419 --> 00:19:16.521
It's a result, in other words.

00:19:16.521 --> 00:19:21.126
- So what would the role
of, in your perfect world,

00:19:21.126 --> 00:19:23.095
what would the role
of Christianity be

00:19:23.095 --> 00:19:25.029
in, say, the United States?

00:19:25.029 --> 00:19:27.933
- Well, I believe in
freedom of religion.

00:19:27.933 --> 00:19:29.400
And I think God does too.

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:32.270 align:start size:97% position:3%
Otherwise, he'd be pestering
us all the time, right?

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:35.707
He gives us enough freedom
in order to go our own way.

00:19:35.707 --> 00:19:38.609
So I think we have
freedom of religion,

00:19:38.609 --> 00:19:42.280 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I would not want any sort
of theocratic government.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:45.383 align:start position:0%
But I think what people
confuse, Dave, on this issue is,

00:19:45.383 --> 00:19:49.121
I just remember, I'll bring
up a name from the past.

00:19:49.121 --> 00:19:51.056
Remember old Chris Matthews?

00:19:51.056 --> 00:19:52.323
- Of course.

00:19:52.323 --> 00:19:54.092
Before MSNBC went
completely insane.

00:19:54.092 --> 00:19:55.427
- (laughs) That's right.

00:19:55.427 --> 00:19:57.229
- He was like kind of
hanging on by a thread.

00:19:57.229 --> 00:19:58.463
- That's right, he used to say,

00:19:58.463 --> 00:20:00.198
"We can't set up some
sort of theocracy."

00:20:00.198 --> 00:20:04.369
And I would say, "Chris,
we're not talking about

00:20:04.369 --> 00:20:07.105 align:start size:97% position:3%
telling people where, when,
how, or if to worship.

00:20:07.105 --> 00:20:09.240 align:start position:0%
We're not telling 'em they
have to be a certain religion."

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:11.109 align:start size:88% position:13%
That would be
establishing a religion.

00:20:11.109 --> 00:20:12.610 align:start size:81% position:19%
But we can't avoid
telling people

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:14.146
how they ought to
treat one another.

00:20:14.146 --> 00:20:15.614
And that's legislating morality,

00:20:15.614 --> 00:20:17.348 align:start size:81% position:19%
and everybody's
trying to do that.

00:20:17.348 --> 00:20:18.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
- It's what the wokesters
are doing right now.

00:20:18.917 --> 00:20:20.118
That was your point.

00:20:20.118 --> 00:20:21.819 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yes, that's what
they're doing.

00:20:21.819 --> 00:20:23.321
The only question
is whose morality?

00:20:23.321 --> 00:20:24.856
So I think,

00:20:24.856 --> 00:20:28.393
and I'll part with some of
my Christian friends here,

00:20:28.393 --> 00:20:31.363 align:start size:88% position:13%
this country was not
founded on Christianity.

00:20:31.363 --> 00:20:32.998
It was founded on the moral law

00:20:32.998 --> 00:20:34.933
consistent with Christianity.

00:20:34.933 --> 00:20:38.003 align:start position:0%
When Jefferson says we hold
these truths to be self-evident,

00:20:38.003 --> 00:20:39.738 align:start size:81% position:19%
that we're endowed
by our creator,

00:20:39.738 --> 00:20:44.176 align:start size:88% position:13%
what he wanted to do was
have a new government

00:20:44.176 --> 00:20:48.146 align:start size:97% position:3%
that wasn't completely
relativistic and have no god,

00:20:48.146 --> 00:20:50.649
but he didn't want a government,

00:20:50.649 --> 00:20:52.050
like they came from
England that said,

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:53.885
you gotta be a member of
the Church of England.

00:20:53.885 --> 00:20:55.587
He wanted to have the
best of both worlds,

00:20:55.587 --> 00:20:58.323
religious freedom but
also moral absolutes

00:20:58.323 --> 00:20:59.591
that come from God.

00:20:59.591 --> 00:21:01.926 align:start size:84% position:16%
So he said, we're
gonna establish this

00:21:01.926 --> 00:21:05.030 align:start size:88% position:13%
on the moral law that
comes from God's nature.

00:21:05.030 --> 00:21:08.300
That moral law we know
comes from the same god

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:10.735
that wrote the Bible
or inspired the Bible.

00:21:10.735 --> 00:21:13.404
But you don't have to be
a Christian to know it.

00:21:13.404 --> 00:21:15.841 align:start position:0%
You don't have to be a
Christian to be a citizen here.

00:21:15.841 --> 00:21:18.276
This nation is open to everyone.

00:21:18.276 --> 00:21:20.178 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's the kind of government
I think we ought to have.

00:21:20.178 --> 00:21:22.614
- Is that the brilliance
of the line self-evident

00:21:22.614 --> 00:21:24.115
more than anything else?

00:21:24.115 --> 00:21:26.685
Because, again, when I quote
this line about Douglas Murray

00:21:26.685 --> 00:21:28.753
that we're debating things

00:21:28.753 --> 00:21:30.288
that shouldn't be
debated anymore.

00:21:30.288 --> 00:21:32.357
It seems to me we're in
this odd spot right now

00:21:32.357 --> 00:21:35.060 align:start size:88% position:13%
where culturally nothing
self-evident anymore.

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:36.861 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's nothing
that's just settled.

00:21:36.861 --> 00:21:38.930
What is settled
anymore? Nothing.

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:41.566
Was JFK assassinated?
Did we go to the moon?

00:21:41.566 --> 00:21:43.268
Are boys girls, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:21:43.268 --> 00:21:45.237 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh, yeah, it is
crazy in that regard.

00:21:45.237 --> 00:21:48.240
And I think part of
that is what happens,

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:49.775
to use a biblical phrase,

00:21:49.775 --> 00:21:52.144
that when you suppress
the truth long enough,

00:21:52.144 --> 00:21:55.047
God gives you up
to a depraved mind.

00:21:55.047 --> 00:21:56.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, to the point
where you don't even know

00:21:56.915 --> 00:21:59.117
there's a difference
between boys and girls.

00:21:59.117 --> 00:22:02.053
By the way, transgenderism
presupposes men and women.

00:22:02.053 --> 00:22:04.589 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because if I'm a man,
and I think I'm a woman,

00:22:04.589 --> 00:22:06.091
I have to have some
idea what a man is

00:22:06.091 --> 00:22:08.259
and some idea what a woman is
to know I have this mismatch

00:22:08.259 --> 00:22:10.562 align:start size:84% position:16%
between my psychology
and my biology.

00:22:10.562 --> 00:22:12.731
If there weren't fixed genders,

00:22:12.731 --> 00:22:14.298
I wouldn't be able to know that.

00:22:14.298 --> 00:22:17.035 align:start size:97% position:3%
Also, if I'm gonna try and
make the so-called transition,

00:22:17.035 --> 00:22:18.570
I have to have some
idea what a man is

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:20.238
and some idea what a woman is

00:22:20.238 --> 00:22:21.673
in order to make the transition.

00:22:21.673 --> 00:22:22.707 align:start size:84% position:16%
So on one hand
they're trying to say,

00:22:22.707 --> 00:22:24.075
"Oh, gender's completely fluid."

00:22:24.075 --> 00:22:25.744
On the other hand, it
would be impossible.

00:22:25.744 --> 00:22:27.446
Transgenders would be impossible

00:22:27.446 --> 00:22:28.913
if there weren't fixed genders.

00:22:28.913 --> 00:22:30.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, it's interesting
'cause one of the things

00:22:30.448 --> 00:22:32.651
I've been talking about on the
show, it's the same argument,

00:22:32.651 --> 00:22:34.152 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've made it slightly
different way,

00:22:34.152 --> 00:22:36.354
which is that with kids,
they're taking a young boy

00:22:36.354 --> 00:22:38.223
who maybe likes the
color pink or Barbie.

00:22:38.223 --> 00:22:41.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
On one hand, they're telling
you none of this matters.

00:22:41.093 --> 00:22:42.527 align:start size:84% position:16%
And on the other
hand they're saying,

00:22:42.527 --> 00:22:43.862 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Oh, he likes pink and Barbie,
thus he must be a girl."

00:22:43.862 --> 00:22:45.029
- That's right.

00:22:45.029 --> 00:22:47.632 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So it's the most
radically authoritarian,

00:22:47.632 --> 00:22:49.901
while it's also saying
it's the most tolerant

00:22:49.901 --> 00:22:51.203
and diverse and everything else,

00:22:51.203 --> 00:22:53.071
which I suppose that
doesn't surprise you

00:22:53.071 --> 00:22:54.206
because it's disconnected from.

00:22:54.206 --> 00:22:55.774
- It is.

00:22:55.774 --> 00:23:00.345
When you move away from God,
even just say a generic god,

00:23:01.646 --> 00:23:05.016
the god of right and wrong,

00:23:05.016 --> 00:23:08.319
when you move away from
that, God's a gentleman.

00:23:08.319 --> 00:23:10.655
He gives up on you.

00:23:10.655 --> 00:23:12.424
Eventually, he says, "I
know you don't want me.

00:23:12.424 --> 00:23:14.226
So I'm gonna pull myself away."

00:23:14.226 --> 00:23:15.493
God is not gonna force you

00:23:15.493 --> 00:23:17.062
into his presence
against your will.

00:23:17.062 --> 00:23:19.431 align:start size:84% position:16%
And from a theological
perspective, that's
what hell is.

00:23:19.431 --> 00:23:20.898
Hell is separation from God

00:23:20.898 --> 00:23:23.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
because God will not force
you into his presence

00:23:23.334 --> 00:23:24.602
against your will.

00:23:24.602 --> 00:23:26.871
I always say, if you
don't want God now,

00:23:26.871 --> 00:23:28.406 align:start size:84% position:16%
you're not gonna
want him in eternity.

00:23:28.406 --> 00:23:31.276
Why would he say, "You're
with me now in eternity?"

00:23:31.276 --> 00:23:35.046
No, he's gonna pull himself
away, and the problem is,

00:23:35.046 --> 00:23:37.815
if this is true, if
Christianity's true,
and I think it is,

00:23:37.815 --> 00:23:42.086 align:start size:97% position:3%
then the worst place you
can be is separated from God

00:23:42.086 --> 00:23:44.455
because he is the
source of all love.

00:23:44.455 --> 00:23:47.024 align:start size:97% position:3%
He is the source of all
goodness. He is the standard.

00:23:47.024 --> 00:23:48.260
I mean, you've been
talking about this.

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:49.761
You've been talking about,

00:23:49.761 --> 00:23:52.197
there's gotta be some sort
of transcendence out there.

00:23:52.197 --> 00:23:53.731 align:start size:84% position:16%
- There has to be
something beyond this.

00:23:53.731 --> 00:23:54.565
- Yeah, that's right.

00:23:54.565 --> 00:23:56.134
- It's not just this.

00:23:56.134 --> 00:23:57.636 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's just not just this.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:23:57.636 --> 00:23:58.904
- I know that much.

00:23:58.904 --> 00:24:00.405
- And maybe that's
part of the reason,

00:24:00.405 --> 00:24:02.006 align:start size:88% position:13%
going back to your
original question, Dave,

00:24:02.006 --> 00:24:05.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the new atheism
doesn't or has faded away

00:24:05.610 --> 00:24:07.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause people know there's
something transcendent.

00:24:07.945 --> 00:24:09.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're not just
molecular machines.

00:24:09.681 --> 00:24:10.949
We're not just moist robots.

00:24:10.949 --> 00:24:13.317
And if we are moist robots,

00:24:13.317 --> 00:24:14.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
why should we believe
atheism is true?

00:24:14.753 --> 00:24:15.987 align:start size:84% position:16%
We shouldn't
believe our thoughts

00:24:15.987 --> 00:24:17.422
because our thoughts
are completely driven

00:24:17.422 --> 00:24:19.658 align:start size:84% position:16%
by the laws of physics
according to them.

00:24:19.658 --> 00:24:23.494 align:start size:84% position:16%
So materialism is
really self-defeating.

00:24:23.494 --> 00:24:25.663
And that's the main
atheistic view now.

00:24:25.663 --> 00:24:27.331
In fact, Thomas Nagle,

00:24:27.331 --> 00:24:31.235 align:start size:88% position:13%
who I think John Lennox
mentioned in your
dialogue with him,

00:24:31.235 --> 00:24:32.970
he's an NYU professor,

00:24:32.970 --> 00:24:36.707
wrote a book about 12 years
ago called "Mind and Cosmos"

00:24:36.707 --> 00:24:39.511
where he said, even though
he's an atheist, he said,

00:24:39.511 --> 00:24:41.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm trying to
remember the subtitle.

00:24:41.479 --> 00:24:43.081
He said something like,

00:24:43.081 --> 00:24:46.551 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Why the Neo Darwinian
materialistic
viewpoint of the world

00:24:46.551 --> 00:24:49.053
is almost certainly false."

00:24:49.053 --> 00:24:51.956 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he got so much pushback
from his fellow atheist

00:24:51.956 --> 00:24:53.558 align:start size:88% position:13%
because he's essentially
saying, "Look,

00:24:53.558 --> 00:24:55.092
I know there's something
transcendent out there.

00:24:55.092 --> 00:24:57.362
I'm an atheist. I don't
know how to explain it."

00:24:57.362 --> 00:24:59.931
But materialism
isn't the answer.

00:24:59.931 --> 00:25:02.267
Materialism makes
us moist robots.

00:25:02.267 --> 00:25:04.102
Why should anyone listen to me?

00:25:04.102 --> 00:25:05.603
Why should I listen to myself?

00:25:05.603 --> 00:25:07.172
- Well, it's just not that
fun. It's not that sellable.

00:25:07.172 --> 00:25:09.007
I think that that's
part of it also.

00:25:09.007 --> 00:25:10.275
There's no imagination there.

00:25:10.275 --> 00:25:11.509
I don't know if you saw it,

00:25:11.509 --> 00:25:13.444
but I got into a bit of
a debate about belief,

00:25:13.444 --> 00:25:14.879
taking the believer
side with Bill Maher

00:25:14.879 --> 00:25:16.915
on his podcast not too long ago.

00:25:16.915 --> 00:25:19.450
And it just struck me as,

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:22.787 align:start size:97% position:3%
oh, you don't believe in any
anything sort of magical,

00:25:22.787 --> 00:25:25.757 align:start position:0%
it's like that's such a
fundamental part of being human.

00:25:25.757 --> 00:25:27.458
Whether we could
whittle this down

00:25:27.458 --> 00:25:29.794
into the firm belief
in Christianity or
any other religion,

00:25:29.794 --> 00:25:33.030
that the need to believe
is just innate to humans.

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:35.233 align:start size:97% position:3%
You will believe in
something one way or another.

00:25:35.233 --> 00:25:36.734 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, and you've
mentioned this,

00:25:36.734 --> 00:25:38.270
and Jordan Peterson
has mentioned this.

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:40.272
Everybody has some kind of god.

00:25:40.272 --> 00:25:42.007
There's a hierarchy of values.

00:25:42.007 --> 00:25:44.242
And there's something at the top

00:25:44.242 --> 00:25:46.711
of all of our priority lists.

00:25:46.711 --> 00:25:48.913
- So what would you
say, so you mentioned

00:25:48.913 --> 00:25:50.882 align:start size:94% position:6%
you've debated Christopher
Hitchens on this.

00:25:50.882 --> 00:25:52.483
When you look at
someone like Hitch

00:25:52.483 --> 00:25:55.920 align:start size:97% position:3%
or you look at say Carl Sagan
or even Albert Einstein,

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:57.154
the people that were sort of,

00:25:57.154 --> 00:25:58.690 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't know that
Einstein was fully not,

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:00.658
he thought that God didn't
play dice with the universe,

00:26:00.658 --> 00:26:01.826
was the famous line.

00:26:01.826 --> 00:26:03.028
But when you look at the people

00:26:03.028 --> 00:26:05.497 align:start size:84% position:16%
that were sort of
not religious per se,

00:26:05.497 --> 00:26:07.432
somewhat separated from belief,

00:26:07.432 --> 00:26:10.501 align:start position:0%
but clearly lived good,
inquisitive, interesting lives,

00:26:10.501 --> 00:26:12.870 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, didn't do harm
as far as I can tell,

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:14.305
something like that,

00:26:14.305 --> 00:26:16.374 align:start size:97% position:3%
what what would you say
about that sort of character,

00:26:16.374 --> 00:26:17.576
that that person.

00:26:17.576 --> 00:26:19.110 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, I've certainly
have always said,

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:21.646
and I said this to Hitchens
several times when I said,

00:26:21.646 --> 00:26:25.050
"Christopher, I'm not saying
that since you're an atheist,

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:26.684
you can't do good things."

00:26:26.684 --> 00:26:28.820 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm not saying you don't
know what right and wrong is.

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:30.087
Everybody does.

00:26:30.087 --> 00:26:31.389
What I'm saying is
you can't justify

00:26:31.389 --> 00:26:32.757
what right and wrong is.

00:26:32.757 --> 00:26:36.427
You can know it,
and you can do it,

00:26:36.427 --> 00:26:39.463 align:start size:84% position:16%
but you can't justify
why it's good.

00:26:39.463 --> 00:26:41.800 align:start size:88% position:13%
If there's no God,
it's just your opinion.

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:43.635
I sometimes give an analogy.

00:26:43.635 --> 00:26:45.670
I notice there's a lot
of speed limit signs

00:26:45.670 --> 00:26:47.105
around here in Miami.

00:26:47.105 --> 00:26:49.207 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Have you seen
these people drive?

00:26:49.207 --> 00:26:50.608 align:start size:88% position:13%
We have a lot of
different cultures here.

00:26:50.608 --> 00:26:52.076
It's a real thing.

00:26:52.076 --> 00:26:53.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
- (laughs) And there's
cameras everywhere anyway.

00:26:53.544 --> 00:26:56.681
You can go outside and see
speed limit 35 miles an hour,

00:26:56.681 --> 00:26:59.384 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you can obey
that speed limit sign

00:26:59.384 --> 00:27:01.853
while you deny there's
a traffic authority.

00:27:01.853 --> 00:27:04.255
But there would be
no speed limit sign

00:27:04.255 --> 00:27:05.890
unless there was a
traffic authority.

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:08.492
The same thing is true with God.

00:27:08.492 --> 00:27:10.327
You can know that
say murder's wrong

00:27:10.327 --> 00:27:14.098
and deny there's a God,
and you can not murder,

00:27:14.098 --> 00:27:16.768
do good things, and
deny there's a God.

00:27:16.768 --> 00:27:18.670 align:start size:81% position:19%
But it wouldn't
be wrong to murder

00:27:18.670 --> 00:27:22.140
from an objective point of
view unless there was a God.

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:25.043
- And what would Hitch's
answer have been on that?

00:27:25.043 --> 00:27:26.310
Or what was it?

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:28.846
- Well, hitch was so
rhetorically gifted,

00:27:28.846 --> 00:27:30.582 align:start size:88% position:13%
he would never directly
answer a question.

00:27:30.582 --> 00:27:32.149
(Dave laughs)

00:27:32.149 --> 00:27:36.153 align:start size:97% position:3%
And here's, look, I don't
try and judge my own debates,

00:27:36.153 --> 00:27:39.457
but I will say this, the
first debate I ever had,

00:27:39.457 --> 00:27:42.694
other than with my wife,
which I lose repeatedly,

00:27:42.694 --> 00:27:44.562
but the first debate I ever had

00:27:44.562 --> 00:27:46.030 align:start size:84% position:16%
was against
Christopher Hitchens.

00:27:46.030 --> 00:27:50.768 align:start size:84% position:16%
And during the debate,
literally, Dave,

00:27:50.768 --> 00:27:54.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was sitting there
going, "I like this guy."

00:27:54.139 --> 00:27:56.040
I didn't even know what
he was talking about

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:57.175
'cause he was all over the map.

00:27:57.175 --> 00:28:00.277
But, you know, just his accent

00:28:00.277 --> 00:28:02.279 align:start size:84% position:16%
and his wit.
- Great way about him.

00:28:02.279 --> 00:28:03.781 align:start size:84% position:16%
- He was just
fabulous, you know,

00:28:03.781 --> 00:28:06.984 align:start position:0%
and I'm kind of daydreaming
going, "I really like this guy."

00:28:06.984 --> 00:28:08.987
And I think people
in the audience,

00:28:08.987 --> 00:28:10.088 align:start size:84% position:16%
when they're
watching the debate,

00:28:10.088 --> 00:28:11.555
they're doing the same thing.

00:28:11.555 --> 00:28:13.891
But if you look at the
transcript of the debate,

00:28:13.891 --> 00:28:15.660
and you're reading
it, you're going,

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:17.361
"What's he talking about?

00:28:17.361 --> 00:28:19.998
This has nothing to do
with even the topic."

00:28:19.998 --> 00:28:23.501
You know, the first debate
was called "Does God Exist?"

00:28:23.501 --> 00:28:26.070
So I got up and I tried to
give some of the arguments

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:27.338
we already mentioned.

00:28:27.338 --> 00:28:28.839
You know, what he got
up and started doing?

00:28:28.839 --> 00:28:32.644
He started talking about how
Mother Teresa was a bad person.

00:28:32.644 --> 00:28:34.345
And I was just baffled and went,

00:28:34.345 --> 00:28:36.314 align:start position:0%
"What does this have to do with
whether or not God exists?"

00:28:36.314 --> 00:28:38.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
Mother Teresa May
have been good or bad.

00:28:38.249 --> 00:28:39.350
That's not the point.

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:40.651
What about God?

00:28:40.651 --> 00:28:43.554 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he just never
engaged on these issues.

00:28:43.554 --> 00:28:44.722 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, unfortunately,
he's not here.

00:28:44.722 --> 00:28:46.624
So we can't fully rehash it.

00:28:46.624 --> 00:28:48.392
But would you say that the flaw,

00:28:48.392 --> 00:28:51.262
if he and some of these
other people I mentioned

00:28:51.262 --> 00:28:53.765 align:start size:94% position:6%
lived roughly decent
lives and everything else,

00:28:53.765 --> 00:28:55.733
would you say that the
flaw is more functional

00:28:55.733 --> 00:28:57.569
in that it just can't scale?

00:28:57.569 --> 00:28:59.103
- What do you mean by that?

00:28:59.103 --> 00:29:02.574
- So it can work for sort of
very, in individual people.

00:29:02.574 --> 00:29:04.075
I have no doubt that you believe

00:29:04.075 --> 00:29:06.110
that an individual atheist
could live a perfectly moral

00:29:06.110 --> 00:29:07.312
and good life.

00:29:07.312 --> 00:29:09.013
- Not perfectly, none
of us, yeah, yeah.

00:29:09.013 --> 00:29:11.549
- Within the construct
of being a human being,

00:29:11.549 --> 00:29:14.385
you could live a fairly
moral and decent life

00:29:14.385 --> 00:29:15.620
and everything else,

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:17.555
but that it just sort of
can't scale over time.

00:29:17.555 --> 00:29:19.157
It just doesn't give you
enough for generations

00:29:19.157 --> 00:29:22.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
to pass on to live in some
sort of functional way.

00:29:22.827 --> 00:29:24.429
- I know it's gonna sound-

00:29:24.429 --> 00:29:25.596
- And I think that's sort of why

00:29:25.596 --> 00:29:27.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
this American atheist
thing collapsed

00:29:27.198 --> 00:29:28.699 align:start size:84% position:16%
and why the movement
has collapsed.

00:29:28.699 --> 00:29:30.268
- But I know it's
gonna sound odd,

00:29:30.268 --> 00:29:31.669
but I don't think
the purpose of life

00:29:31.669 --> 00:29:33.371
is to live a moral life.

00:29:33.371 --> 00:29:35.072 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think the purpose
of life is to know God

00:29:35.072 --> 00:29:36.707
and make him known.

00:29:36.707 --> 00:29:39.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if you do do that, you
will live a moral life.

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:41.245
In other words, it's a result.

00:29:41.245 --> 00:29:42.947
It is not the cause.

00:29:42.947 --> 00:29:46.784 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because we're all fallen,
and if God is God,

00:29:46.784 --> 00:29:49.987
which means he's
infinitely just,

00:29:49.987 --> 00:29:53.391
I've been unjust in
my life even today,

00:29:53.391 --> 00:29:54.858
just coming over here.

00:29:54.858 --> 00:29:57.061 align:start size:97% position:3%
How many people did I cut
off 'cause I was late, right?

00:29:57.061 --> 00:29:59.430
Okay, I'm unjust every day.

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:00.932
And if God is infinitely just,

00:30:00.932 --> 00:30:03.667
at the judgment, I'm in trouble.

00:30:03.667 --> 00:30:06.704 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so I need somebody
to cover my sin,

00:30:06.704 --> 00:30:09.040
somebody to pay for my sin.

00:30:09.040 --> 00:30:10.741
An unpopular word
in today's culture,

00:30:10.741 --> 00:30:15.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it's transgression
against God or other people.

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:17.681
I've done that.

00:30:17.681 --> 00:30:19.217
I do it all the time.

00:30:19.217 --> 00:30:21.285
So I need somebody
to cover my sin.

00:30:21.285 --> 00:30:23.021
That's why Jesus came.

00:30:23.021 --> 00:30:24.922 align:start size:84% position:16%
The son of man did
not come to be served,

00:30:24.922 --> 00:30:28.793
but to serve and to give his
life as a ransom for many.

00:30:28.793 --> 00:30:31.429 align:start size:97% position:3%
And this is the only
religion in the world, Dave,

00:30:31.429 --> 00:30:36.134
that actually is built on
grace rather than works.

00:30:36.134 --> 00:30:40.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
In other words, you don't
earn favor with God.

00:30:40.605 --> 00:30:42.974
He just gives it to you.

00:30:42.974 --> 00:30:44.976 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then because
he's given it to you,

00:30:44.976 --> 00:30:49.881
then you live a life
out of gratitude to him

00:30:49.881 --> 00:30:52.584
that will live in a moral way.

00:30:52.584 --> 00:30:54.686 align:start size:84% position:16%
You will live in a
moral way, that's the.

00:30:54.686 --> 00:30:56.221
- So connected to that,

00:30:56.221 --> 00:30:58.223
how much of what you think is
happening sort of culturally

00:30:58.223 --> 00:31:00.191
in America right now and
really all over the world.

00:31:00.191 --> 00:31:03.193 align:start size:97% position:3%
As you may know, I just
spent about 12 days in Israel

00:31:03.193 --> 00:31:05.363
and went, you know, to
all of the holy sites

00:31:05.363 --> 00:31:06.698 align:start size:84% position:16%
and went to the Church
of Holy Sepulcher

00:31:06.698 --> 00:31:07.932
and went to the Western Wall.

00:31:07.932 --> 00:31:10.101
- You did some college
work there, didn't you?

00:31:10.101 --> 00:31:12.170
In the Negev, a semester there?

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:15.472
- Yeah, that was 20 something
years ago, a long time ago.

00:31:15.472 --> 00:31:19.477
But one of the things that
struck me was that the story,

00:31:19.477 --> 00:31:20.844
it was very clear,

00:31:20.844 --> 00:31:22.981 align:start size:97% position:3%
especially being in Jerusalem
for about five days,

00:31:22.981 --> 00:31:24.482 align:start size:84% position:16%
that, you know,
they're digging down

00:31:24.482 --> 00:31:26.517
and excavating and
uncovering history,

00:31:26.517 --> 00:31:27.785
and then they're
also building up.

00:31:27.785 --> 00:31:29.687
I mean, the city is just
absolutely flourishing.

00:31:29.687 --> 00:31:31.522 align:start size:88% position:13%
It was amazing, but what
I kept thinking was,

00:31:31.522 --> 00:31:33.323
people don't know history

00:31:33.323 --> 00:31:35.560 align:start position:0%
and because they don't know
history, they can't even connect

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:38.496 align:start size:84% position:16%
any of the philosophic
underpinnings.

00:31:38.496 --> 00:31:42.667
So we just sort of all walk
around spinning all day long,

00:31:42.667 --> 00:31:45.737
and we need to know history
to know some of this stuff.

00:31:45.737 --> 00:31:46.804
- Oh, absolutely.

00:31:46.804 --> 00:31:48.806
We go to Israel every year.

00:31:48.806 --> 00:31:51.175
We bring a group
to Israel, in fact.

00:31:51.175 --> 00:31:54.078
Ellie Schucron, who
is the archeologist

00:31:54.078 --> 00:31:56.848
who discovered the Pool of
Siloam and excavated most it.

00:31:56.848 --> 00:31:57.916
- Well, we went down there.

00:31:57.916 --> 00:31:59.317
It's being excavated right now.

00:31:59.317 --> 00:32:01.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, well, he
excavated it back in 2004.

00:32:01.352 --> 00:32:03.420 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that church there,
believe it or not,

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:05.222 align:start size:88% position:13%
didn't want him to
continue the excavation,

00:32:05.222 --> 00:32:08.058 align:start position:0%
and somehow they just got
approval last year to finish it.

00:32:08.058 --> 00:32:11.462 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so Ellie, who was the
guy originally discovered it,

00:32:11.462 --> 00:32:13.096 align:start size:84% position:16%
is normally our tour
guide when we go,

00:32:13.096 --> 00:32:15.867 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're gonna go in November
again, and he discovered that.

00:32:15.867 --> 00:32:18.536 align:start size:81% position:19%
He also discovered
what might be,

00:32:18.536 --> 00:32:22.740
and this is an amazing
discovery if it's true.

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:24.441
I've seen it myself.

00:32:24.441 --> 00:32:29.013 align:start size:97% position:3%
He has found a standing
stone in the City of David,

00:32:29.013 --> 00:32:33.284
which goes all the way back
potentially to Melchizedek.

00:32:33.284 --> 00:32:34.785
Now this is Abraham's time.

00:32:34.785 --> 00:32:36.820
So this is 2000 BC,

00:32:36.820 --> 00:32:40.558
and that's right there
in the City of David.

00:32:40.558 --> 00:32:43.361
This is a thousand
years before David.

00:32:43.361 --> 00:32:46.063
So he's an amazing archeologist.

00:32:46.063 --> 00:32:49.667 align:start size:97% position:3%
And there's no question
this was the Jewish homeland

00:32:49.667 --> 00:32:53.271
long before anybody
else ever got there,

00:32:53.271 --> 00:32:56.174 align:start size:94% position:6%
other than the Canaanites
who were there,

00:32:56.174 --> 00:32:57.608
you know, prior to them.

00:32:57.608 --> 00:32:59.043
So this whole political argument

00:32:59.043 --> 00:33:01.879 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think is just
overcome by archeology.

00:33:01.879 --> 00:33:03.580
- When you're down there,

00:33:03.580 --> 00:33:05.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so we did the walk
through the City of David,

00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:07.151
and they're excavating it now.

00:33:07.151 --> 00:33:09.286
You can go basically from
the pool all the way up

00:33:09.286 --> 00:33:11.089
to what would've
been the temple.

00:33:11.089 --> 00:33:12.590
- Ellie excavated that too.

00:33:12.590 --> 00:33:13.424
Yeah, yeah.
- Right.

00:33:13.424 --> 00:33:14.858
So when you're there,

00:33:14.858 --> 00:33:16.427 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're with the
archeologist and all that,

00:33:16.427 --> 00:33:18.396 align:start size:88% position:13%
are most of the
archeologists believers,

00:33:18.396 --> 00:33:20.364
or are they more purely people-

00:33:20.364 --> 00:33:22.934
- Ellie is certainly
a believer in Yahweh.

00:33:22.934 --> 00:33:25.502
He's a Orthodox Jewish believer.

00:33:25.502 --> 00:33:28.406
I don't know, I can't
speak for the others.

00:33:28.406 --> 00:33:30.241
I just know Ellie personally.

00:33:31.208 --> 00:33:33.310 align:start size:88% position:13%
But there are
Americans, as you know,

00:33:33.310 --> 00:33:35.446
that go over there quite
a bit that do excavate,

00:33:35.446 --> 00:33:36.981
guys like Scott Stripling,

00:33:36.981 --> 00:33:40.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
who just discovered one
of the oldest inscriptions

00:33:40.818 --> 00:33:43.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
ever found in Israel
up in Shechem,

00:33:43.521 --> 00:33:47.491 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is Mount Ebal
next to Mount Gerizim.

00:33:47.491 --> 00:33:50.595
And he thinks he's found
that near Joshua's altar.

00:33:50.595 --> 00:33:53.865
So this would be, say 1400 BC

00:33:53.865 --> 00:33:56.100
or 1200 BC, somewhere in there.

00:33:56.100 --> 00:33:59.871 align:start size:84% position:16%
He's a Christian, and
he excavates quite
a bit over there.

00:33:59.871 --> 00:34:00.905
There are several other guys.

00:34:00.905 --> 00:34:03.607
But as you know, Israel is,

00:34:03.607 --> 00:34:07.445
every time you stick
a spade in the ground,

00:34:07.445 --> 00:34:09.313
there's another discovery.

00:34:09.313 --> 00:34:11.715
- There's 10 different
layers of something.

00:34:11.715 --> 00:34:12.616
There's a layer cake.

00:34:12.616 --> 00:34:13.851
They described it to us

00:34:13.851 --> 00:34:16.821
on our walk of City of
David as a tiramisu.

00:34:16.821 --> 00:34:17.822
Basically, you're
just going through,

00:34:17.822 --> 00:34:19.023
and there's just another layer

00:34:19.023 --> 00:34:21.225
and another layer of
ash, and they can just,

00:34:21.225 --> 00:34:22.593
it's incredible.
- It's amazing.

00:34:22.593 --> 00:34:23.928
- So would you say,
so as a Christian,

00:34:23.928 --> 00:34:25.830
would you say that Christianity

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:29.066
then in essence is
sort of part two

00:34:29.066 --> 00:34:31.335 align:start size:88% position:13%
or just the continuation
of the story

00:34:31.335 --> 00:34:33.137 align:start size:81% position:19%
that the Jewish
Bible came out of,

00:34:33.137 --> 00:34:35.339
that that sort of didn't end

00:34:35.339 --> 00:34:38.475
purely with the ending
of the Old Testament.

00:34:38.475 --> 00:34:41.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right, the Old
Testament prefigures Jesus

00:34:41.178 --> 00:34:42.880
and predicts Jesus.

00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:46.083 align:start position:0%
One of the most astounding
prophecies in the Old Testament,

00:34:46.083 --> 00:34:48.953 align:start size:97% position:3%
of course, is Isaiah 53, the
suffering servant passage

00:34:48.953 --> 00:34:51.455
written 700 years
before Christ came.

00:34:51.455 --> 00:34:53.390
It talks about this servant

00:34:53.390 --> 00:34:56.393 align:start size:84% position:16%
taking our iniquities
on himself,

00:34:56.393 --> 00:34:59.163
that he is the lamb that
goes to the slaughter.

00:34:59.163 --> 00:35:01.131
And so when you see that written

00:35:01.131 --> 00:35:03.467 align:start size:94% position:6%
700 years before he came.
- Before Jesus.

00:35:03.467 --> 00:35:05.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, you go, wow,
there's something to this.

00:35:05.303 --> 00:35:06.770
And there's other prophecies.

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:08.005
- So you think that
was like a nugget,

00:35:08.005 --> 00:35:09.874
or what would they in a
video game, it's like,

00:35:09.874 --> 00:35:11.141
what do they call
it in a video game?

00:35:11.141 --> 00:35:13.044
Like the secret thing in
a video game, they give?

00:35:13.044 --> 00:35:13.978
An Easter egg.

00:35:13.978 --> 00:35:15.079
- Oh, it's an Easter egg.

00:35:15.079 --> 00:35:16.813
Yeah, we found it. There it is.

00:35:16.813 --> 00:35:18.382 align:start size:94% position:6%
- You know, and then they
know that it's there.

00:35:18.382 --> 00:35:20.318 align:start size:84% position:16%
So how do you combine
those two things?

00:35:20.318 --> 00:35:22.086
Obviously Jews still exist.

00:35:22.086 --> 00:35:23.620
Israel still exists.

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:26.089
- Of course, and there's a
future for Israel as well.

00:35:26.089 --> 00:35:29.760
And it's amazing when you think
about it, as you know, Dave,

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:32.329 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's no country in
the history of the world

00:35:32.329 --> 00:35:35.232
that left its homeland
for nearly 2,000 years,

00:35:35.232 --> 00:35:37.868 align:start size:88% position:13%
came back to it speaking
the same language.

00:35:37.868 --> 00:35:39.436
How does that happen?

00:35:39.436 --> 00:35:42.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
And in Isaiah, I
think it's Isaiah 11,

00:35:42.173 --> 00:35:44.342
he says he's gonna
bring the nation

00:35:44.342 --> 00:35:46.944
into the land a second time.

00:35:46.944 --> 00:35:48.979
1948, it happened.

00:35:48.979 --> 00:35:50.480
So.

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:53.583
And you've pointed out,
others have pointed out,

00:35:53.583 --> 00:35:58.656 align:start size:97% position:3%
think about how much land
Arabs have in the Middle East.

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:02.760 align:start size:84% position:16%
We've got New
Jersey for the Jews,

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:05.997
and 20% of them
are Arab citizens

00:36:05.997 --> 00:36:07.999
that have full voting rights.

00:36:07.999 --> 00:36:10.201
So I'm having a hard time

00:36:10.201 --> 00:36:14.004 align:start size:84% position:16%
figuring out why
certain people there

00:36:14.004 --> 00:36:15.739
don't want the Jews
to have a homeland.

00:36:15.739 --> 00:36:17.741
In fact, my friend Michael
Brown has put it this way,

00:36:17.741 --> 00:36:21.812 align:start size:88% position:13%
or maybe somebody, maybe
attributed Dr. Brown.

00:36:21.812 --> 00:36:24.916
He says this, if
the Palestinians,

00:36:24.916 --> 00:36:29.186 align:start size:88% position:13%
or if the Israelites
laid down their weapons,

00:36:29.186 --> 00:36:32.023
there would be no more Israel.

00:36:32.023 --> 00:36:34.191 align:start size:88% position:13%
If the Palestinians
laid down their weapons,

00:36:34.191 --> 00:36:35.626
there'd be no more war.

00:36:35.626 --> 00:36:36.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, I think Goldammer
may have said that.

00:36:36.961 --> 00:36:38.896
- Yeah, maybe.
- Years ago.

00:36:38.896 --> 00:36:40.398
So we only have about
five minutes left.

00:36:40.398 --> 00:36:41.765
So this went by very quickly.

00:36:41.765 --> 00:36:44.168
So how can we wrap
this all up in a way

00:36:44.168 --> 00:36:47.471 align:start size:97% position:3%
that gives the average person
who's watching my show,

00:36:47.471 --> 00:36:48.739
I think my show,

00:36:48.739 --> 00:36:49.974
one of the things that
I'm very proud of is,

00:36:49.974 --> 00:36:51.042
I think we have a
nice cross section.

00:36:51.042 --> 00:36:52.176
- You do.

00:36:52.176 --> 00:36:53.577 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Of sort of atheists
and believers

00:36:53.577 --> 00:36:55.446 align:start size:88% position:13%
and people of all
different walks of life.

00:36:55.446 --> 00:36:56.947
What would be the main thing

00:36:56.947 --> 00:36:59.016
that you think would sort of
unite them in all of this,

00:36:59.016 --> 00:37:01.319 align:start size:97% position:3%
whether they're fully a
believer or fully a Christian,

00:37:01.319 --> 00:37:02.920 align:start size:84% position:16%
or they come more from
the secular world,

00:37:02.920 --> 00:37:04.387
or whatever it might be?

00:37:04.387 --> 00:37:05.889 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, I guess the one
question I would ask people,

00:37:05.889 --> 00:37:07.491
and I do this on
college campuses

00:37:07.491 --> 00:37:09.293 align:start size:88% position:13%
because I speak on a
lot of college campuses,

00:37:09.293 --> 00:37:10.494
and then we have open mic.

00:37:10.494 --> 00:37:11.729 align:start size:81% position:19%
The question I
always ask people.

00:37:11.729 --> 00:37:13.697
- You don' get shouted
off the college campuses

00:37:13.697 --> 00:37:16.400
with all this radical
stuff you're speaking?

00:37:16.400 --> 00:37:18.402
- Well, because I'm
giving the evidence

00:37:18.402 --> 00:37:19.870
that Christianity's true,

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:22.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not necessarily given
a political message,

00:37:22.839 --> 00:37:25.910
although I talk about
politics all the time,

00:37:25.910 --> 00:37:27.544 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's not the focus
of what I'm doing.

00:37:27.544 --> 00:37:29.012 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, my friend
Charlie Kirk,

00:37:29.012 --> 00:37:31.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'm sure you and others,
you'll get shouted down.

00:37:31.282 --> 00:37:33.617 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I've been shouted
down with Charlie Kirk.

00:37:33.617 --> 00:37:35.152
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:37:35.152 --> 00:37:37.554 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so you guys
will take more heat

00:37:37.554 --> 00:37:39.256
because of the angle
you're coming from.

00:37:39.256 --> 00:37:41.559
It's probably gonna
happen soon to me,

00:37:41.559 --> 00:37:43.794
but anyway, on a college campus,

00:37:43.794 --> 00:37:45.362
if someone gets up
to the microphone

00:37:45.362 --> 00:37:47.097
and expresses any
hostility at all,

00:37:47.097 --> 00:37:48.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
I normally stop and
ask this one question.

00:37:48.832 --> 00:37:50.134 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'll say, "If
Christianity were true,

00:37:50.134 --> 00:37:51.735
would you become a Christian?"

00:37:51.735 --> 00:37:54.338 align:start size:97% position:3%
And, Dave, I've had atheists
stand at that microphone

00:37:54.338 --> 00:37:57.341
in front of hundreds
of people and say no.

00:37:57.341 --> 00:37:58.808
Say no? Wait, wait, wait.

00:37:58.808 --> 00:38:00.444
I thought you claimed to
be reasonable and rational.

00:38:00.444 --> 00:38:02.279
How is it reasonable you
wouldn't believe something

00:38:02.279 --> 00:38:03.214
if it were true?

00:38:03.214 --> 00:38:04.848
Well, it's not about reason.

00:38:04.848 --> 00:38:06.984 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's not about the mind,
it's about the heart.

00:38:06.984 --> 00:38:08.619
They don't want it to be true.

00:38:08.619 --> 00:38:10.120 align:start size:81% position:19%
They don't want
there to be a God.

00:38:10.120 --> 00:38:12.556 align:start size:94% position:6%
Why? Because they wanna
be god of their own lives.

00:38:12.556 --> 00:38:15.493
They're not on a truth quest,
they're on a happiness quest.

00:38:15.493 --> 00:38:16.760
And they're just gonna do

00:38:16.760 --> 00:38:18.028
whatever they think is
gonna make them happy.

00:38:18.028 --> 00:38:19.496
And here's the problem.

00:38:19.496 --> 00:38:21.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
You can make yourself
happy over the short term

00:38:21.999 --> 00:38:24.601
doing a lot of fun
but selfish things.

00:38:24.601 --> 00:38:27.604
However, over the long
term, it's a disaster.

00:38:27.604 --> 00:38:31.575 align:start position:0%
And most of us that have passed
40 begin to realize this.

00:38:31.575 --> 00:38:33.944
We go, I just can't live
for myself all the time.

00:38:33.944 --> 00:38:36.280 align:start size:97% position:3%
If I do that, I'm never gonna
have a good relationship.

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:40.017
I'm never gonna find what
really is right about life.

00:38:40.017 --> 00:38:41.585
There's gonna be trouble.

00:38:41.585 --> 00:38:43.420
And what I say to people is,

00:38:43.420 --> 00:38:45.122
look, if you want
true contentment,

00:38:45.122 --> 00:38:46.757 align:start size:84% position:16%
you gotta go straight
through truth.

00:38:46.757 --> 00:38:49.293 align:start size:84% position:16%
And Jesus is the
truth. Check it out.

00:38:49.293 --> 00:38:51.729 align:start size:94% position:6%
The problem is most
people are looking for God

00:38:51.729 --> 00:38:54.431 align:start size:94% position:6%
like a criminal's
looking for a cop, right?

00:38:54.431 --> 00:38:57.501 align:start size:97% position:3%
They want to go their own way,
and so God is a gentleman.

00:38:57.501 --> 00:38:59.403
He lets people go their own way.

00:38:59.403 --> 00:39:01.438
But I think he gives us,
let me sum it up this way.

00:39:01.438 --> 00:39:04.942 align:start size:97% position:3%
He gives us enough evidence
to know that he exists,

00:39:04.942 --> 00:39:06.277
and he gives us enough evidence

00:39:06.277 --> 00:39:08.379
to know how he wants us to live,

00:39:08.379 --> 00:39:11.081 align:start size:84% position:16%
but not so much that
we can't be free

00:39:11.081 --> 00:39:13.651
and go our own way
if we don't want to.

00:39:13.651 --> 00:39:15.352 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's what a
gentleman does, right?

00:39:15.352 --> 00:39:17.387
- It kind of sounds like
the message of America,

00:39:17.387 --> 00:39:18.655
if I'm not mistaken.

00:39:18.655 --> 00:39:21.258 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, well, the
founders knew, Dave.

00:39:21.258 --> 00:39:25.428
I mean, this three branch system

00:39:25.428 --> 00:39:29.867 align:start size:84% position:16%
where the legislature
is supposed to be
the superior branch.

00:39:29.867 --> 00:39:33.170 align:start size:84% position:16%
They don't exercise
that very well.

00:39:33.170 --> 00:39:34.772
But the founders knew

00:39:34.772 --> 00:39:38.642
that human beings are
inherently selfish.

00:39:38.642 --> 00:39:43.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
As Madison said,
"If men were angels,

00:39:43.480 --> 00:39:46.751
no government would
be necessary," right?

00:39:46.751 --> 00:39:48.819 align:start size:88% position:13%
They knew that we needed
checks and balances

00:39:48.819 --> 00:39:51.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
because of the
fallen human heart.

00:39:51.521 --> 00:39:55.259
Yet the left now, they think
people are inherently good.

00:39:55.259 --> 00:39:56.760 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's the fundamental,
in my view,

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:58.295 align:start size:84% position:16%
the fundamental
problem with leftism.

00:39:58.295 --> 00:40:00.297 align:start size:84% position:16%
They think people
are inherently good,

00:40:00.297 --> 00:40:04.034 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they don't need
incentives to stay in line.

00:40:04.034 --> 00:40:07.304 align:start size:97% position:3%
They're just gonna do the
right thing. No, they're not.

00:40:07.304 --> 00:40:09.840
- We're imperfect people. We
can't create a perfect system.

00:40:09.840 --> 00:40:11.141 align:start size:84% position:16%
- [Frank] That's
right, that's right.

00:40:11.141 --> 00:40:12.343
- Frank, I wish
we had more time.

00:40:12.343 --> 00:40:13.277
I really do. It was a pleasure.

00:40:13.277 --> 00:40:14.445
- Brother, God bless you.

00:40:14.445 --> 00:40:15.345
Thank you for having me on.

00:40:15.345 --> 00:40:16.814
